Pipe and Socket Creation: Power-On to Userspace
Stage 1: Firmware / Pre-Boot (BIOS/UEFI)

No pipes or sockets exist yet. Hardware is enumerated, POST runs, boot device selected. The CPU is in real mode (x86). Nothing in the IPC subsystem exists — no kernel, no memory allocator, no VFS.
Stage 2: Bootloader

GRUB2 / LILO / syslinux loads the compressed kernel image (vmlinuz) and optional initrd/initramfs into physical RAM, then jumps to the kernel entry point (arch/x86/boot/header.S → startup_32 / startup_64).

Still no pipes. No sockets. The kernel has not initialized anything yet.
Stage 3: Kernel Decompression and Early Init

Files: arch/x86/boot/compressed/head_64.S, arch/x86/kernel/head_64.S

    startup_64 sets up page tables, enables long mode, jumps to start_kernel() in init/main.c
    start_kernel() calls a strict ordered sequence — nothing IPC-related fires until memory and the VFS are ready

Stage 4: Memory Allocator Init

Files: mm/page_alloc.c, mm/slab.c / mm/slub.c

start_kernel() calls:

    setup_arch() — detects RAM, builds physical memory map
    build_all_zonelists() — sets up NUMA zones
    mem_init() — hands all pages to the buddy allocator
    kmem_cache_init() — brings up the SLAB/SLUB allocator

This is a prerequisite. Every pipe buffer, socket structure, inode, and file descriptor will be allocated from these caches. Without this stage, nothing else runs.
Stage 5: VFS Init — Where Inodes and Files Become Possible

Files: fs/dcache.c, fs/inode.c, fs/file_table.c

start_kernel() calls vfs_caches_init():

vfs_caches_init()
  ├── dcache_init()         — dentry cache (kmem_cache: dentry)
  ├── inode_init()          — inode cache (kmem_cache: inode_cache)
  ├── files_init()          — file struct cache (kmem_cache: filp)
  ├── mnt_init()            — mount namespace, rootfs mount
  └── bdev_cache_init()     — block device inode cache

After mnt_init(), a rootfs tmpfs mount exists in memory. The VFS is now functional. Inodes can be allocated. File descriptors can be opened.

Pipes become structurally possible here, because a pipe is a VFS object (two file descriptors + an inode on pipefs).
Stage 6: pipefs Registration

File: fs/pipe.c
c

static int __init init_pipe_fs(void)
{
    register_filesystem(&pipe_fs_type);
    pipe_mnt = kern_mount(&pipe_fs_type);
    ...
}
fs_initcall(init_pipe_fs);

fs_initcall macros are executed during do_initcalls() inside start_kernel() → do_basic_setup(). This happens during early kernel init, before any userspace process runs.

After this call:

    pipefs is mounted internally (not visible to userspace)
    pipe_inode_info slab cache exists
    pipe_read_open, pipe_write_open, pipe_rdwr_ops file operations are registered
    The kernel pipe() syscall handler (fs/pipe.c: do_pipe_flags()) can now create functional pipes

Pipes are now creatable from kernel context.
Stage 7: Socket Subsystem Init

File: net/socket.c
c

static int __init sock_init(void)
{
    skb_init();           // sk_buff slab cache
    init_inodecache();    // sockfs inode cache
    register_filesystem(&sock_fs_type);
    sock_mnt = kern_mount(&sock_fs_type);
    ...
}
core_initcall(sock_init);

core_initcall runs before fs_initcall, so sockfs is initialized prior to pipefs.

After this:

    sockfs is internally mounted
    socket_alloc slab cache exists
    sock_create_kern() can create kernel-internal sockets
    The socket() syscall handler is wired

Individual protocol families register separately via proto_register() and sock_register():
Protocol	File	initcall level
AF_UNIX	net/unix/af_unix.c	fs_initcall(af_unix_init)
AF_INET (TCP/UDP)	net/ipv4/af_inet.c	fs_initcall(inet_init)
AF_INET6	net/ipv6/af_inet6.c	module_init or fs_initcall
AF_NETLINK	net/netlink/af_netlink.c	core_initcall
AF_PACKET	net/packet/af_packet.c	fs_initcall

AF_UNIX is the critical one — it's the socket type used for all local IPC (systemd, DBus, X11, etc.) and it's ready before PID 1 starts.
Stage 8: Network Stack Init

Files: net/core/dev.c, net/ipv4/tcp.c, net/ipv4/udp.c

inet_init()
  ├── proto_register(&tcp_prot)
  ├── proto_register(&udp_prot)
  ├── proto_register(&raw_prot)
  ├── sock_register(&inet_family_ops)   ← AF_INET now usable
  ├── ip_init()
  ├── tcp_init()                        ← TCP hash tables, slab caches
  └── udp_init()                        ← UDP hash tables

After inet_init(), AF_INET TCP and UDP sockets are fully creatable from kernel space.
Stage 9: do_initcalls() — Full Init Ordering

File: init/main.c

The kernel executes initcall levels in strict order:

early_initcall      — arch setup
pure_initcall       — (rare)
core_initcall       — sock_init(), netlink
postcore_initcall   — bus subsystems
arch_initcall       — arch-specific
subsys_initcall     — device model, PCI
fs_initcall         — init_pipe_fs(), af_unix_init(), inet_init()
rootfs_initcall     — populate rootfs
device_initcall     — most drivers
late_initcall       — optional/deferred

By the end of do_initcalls(), all of the following are ready:

    pipefs mounted, pipe() fully operational
    sockfs mounted
    AF_UNIX, AF_INET, AF_NETLINK, AF_PACKET all registered

Stage 10: kernel_init() — PID 1 Fork

File: init/main.c
c

kernel_thread(kernel_init, NULL, CLONE_FS);

The idle thread (PID 0) spawns kernel_init as PID 1. This thread:

    Calls kernel_init_freeable() — completes driver init, mounts devtmpfs
    Opens /dev/console as fds 0, 1, 2
    Calls run_init_process() → execve("/sbin/init") (or systemd)

At the execve point, userspace begins. The kernel's FD table for PID 1 now has three entries. No pipes yet in userspace — just the console.
Stage 11: initramfs / initrd Execution

If initramfs is used:

The kernel unpacks the CPIO archive into rootfs and executes /init (a shell script or binary). This script:

    May create pipes via shell (| operators) — these call pipe2() syscall
    Mounts /proc, /sys, /dev
    May bring up udevd — which uses AF_NETLINK sockets to receive kernel uevents
    Pivots root and execs real init

First userspace pipes typically appear here in the initramfs /init shell pipelines.
Stage 12: systemd / SysVinit — First Durable Sockets

systemd (/usr/lib/systemd/systemd, PID 1 after pivot):
Socket Units (.socket files)

systemd reads unit files from:

    /usr/lib/systemd/system/
    /etc/systemd/system/

For each .socket unit, systemd calls socket() + bind() + listen() before starting the associated service. This is socket activation.

Critical early sockets created by systemd:
Socket path	Type	Purpose
/run/systemd/private/	AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM	Internal systemd IPC
/run/udev/control	AF_UNIX SOCK_SEQPACKET	udevd control
/run/systemd/journal/socket	AF_UNIX SOCK_DGRAM	journald early logging
/run/systemd/journal/stdout	AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM	stdout capture
/run/dbus/system_bus_socket	AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM	D-Bus system bus

These are created by systemd's own socket_address_listen() before any service process spawns.
Pipes in systemd

systemd creates pipe2(O_CLOEXEC) pairs internally for:

    stdout/stderr capture of service processes
    Watchdog communication
    Synchronization barriers during service startup

File: src/basic/pipe.c in systemd source
Stage 13: Userspace Syscall Paths (Runtime)

Once userspace is running, all pipes and sockets are created through standard syscalls:
pipe()

userspace: pipe(fds) or pipe2(fds, flags)
    │
    └─► sys_pipe2()                   [fs/pipe.c]
          ├── get_pipe_inode()        ← allocates inode on pipefs
          │     └── new_inode_pseudo(pipe_mnt->mnt_sb)
          ├── alloc_pipe_info()       ← allocates pipe_inode_info
          │     └── kcalloc(pipe_bufs, sizeof(struct pipe_buffer))
          ├── create_pipe_files()     ← two struct file*, one R one W
          └── get_unused_fd_flags() x2 ← installs into FD table

A pipe is: 2 file descriptors + 1 pipefs inode + 1 pipe_inode_info + N pipe_buffer structs (default 16 pages = 64KB).
socket()

userspace: socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0)
    │
    └─► sys_socket()                  [net/socket.c]
          ├── sock_create()
          │     ├── security_socket_create()   ← LSM hook (SELinux/AppArmor)
          │     ├── net_proto_family lookup    ← finds inet_family_ops
          │     └── inet_create()             [net/ipv4/af_inet.c]
          │           ├── sk_alloc()          ← allocates sock struct from proto slab
          │           ├── inet_sock_destruct setup
          │           └── proto->init()       ← tcp_v4_init_sock() for TCP
          └── sock_map_fd()
                ├── get_unused_fd_flags()
                ├── sock_alloc_file()         ← creates struct file on sockfs
                └── fd_install()              ← installs into FD table

A socket is: 1 file descriptor + 1 sockfs inode + 1 socket struct + 1 sock struct (protocol-specific, e.g. tcp_sock).
Critical File Map Summary
Subsystem	Kernel Source File	What it does
Pipe filesystem	fs/pipe.c	pipefs registration, pipe() syscall implementation
Pipe buffers	fs/pipe.c	alloc_pipe_info(), pipe_buffer management
Socket core	net/socket.c	sockfs, socket() syscall, sock_create()
AF_UNIX	net/unix/af_unix.c	Local domain sockets
AF_INET	net/ipv4/af_inet.c	TCP/UDP socket creation
