.S Files and Assembly Input Timing
What Language is .S

.S files are GNU Assembler (GAS) source — AT&T syntax x86 assembly, preprocessed by the C preprocessor (cpp) before assembly. The capital .S means cpp runs first (so #include, #define, #ifdef work). Lowercase .s skips cpp.

The syntax is AT&T, not Intel:
asm

movq %rsp, %rbp       # src, dst  (opposite of Intel)
movq $0x1000, %rax    # immediate prefix $
leaq -0x20(%rbp), %rsi # memory operand with base+offset

These files run on bare metal with no C runtime, no stack in some cases, no allocator, no OS — they are what builds the environment that C eventually runs inside.
The Assembly Stages at Boot

head_64.S        ← CPU starts here. Real mode → protected → long mode
compressed/head_64.S  ← decompresses vmlinuz into RAM
vmlinux links    ← .S init sections run before any C function
start_kernel()   ← first C function called from assembly

The .S files set up:

    The GDT and IDT (descriptor tables)
    Paging / page tables
    The stack pointer (%rsp) — C cannot run without this
    CPU feature detection (CPUID)
    BSS zeroing
    Then a call start_kernel or jmp start_kernel

C is helpless until the .S bootstrap hands it a valid stack and memory model.
When Can the System Accept User Assembly?

This is the sharp question. There are several distinct points:
Point 1: Never Before the Bootloader

Before the bootloader, the machine is executing firmware (UEFI/BIOS) from ROM. User input doesn't exist. No keyboard driver, no bus enumeration for input devices — nothing.
Point 2: Bootloader Stage (GRUB rescue shell)

GRUB2 has a minimal command interpreter. If boot fails and you drop to the GRUB rescue shell:

grub rescue>

You can type GRUB commands — not assembly. No assembler is present. The earliest "user input that affects execution" is here, but it's GRUB scripting, not assembly.
Point 3: initramfs Shell — First Real User Input

When initramfs drops a shell (busybox ash), you have:

    A working keyboard driver
    A tty
    A shell

But no assembler is present in a minimal initramfs. You can write shell, not assembly.
Point 4: Userspace with an Assembler Installed

The absolute earliest you can submit assembly for execution:

Option A — as (GNU assembler) in userspace:
bash

echo "movl \$1, %eax" | as -o /tmp/t.o   # needs full rootfs mounted

Available once the root filesystem is mounted and binutils is present.

Option B — nasm / yasm — same requirement, full userspace.

Option C — inline assembly via gcc:
c

asm volatile("nop");

Compiled in userspace, same timing.
The Earliest Possible Path to User-Supplied Assembly Execution

If you want assembly executing as early as physically possible from user input, the answer is:

Replace or modify the initramfs.

The initramfs is a CPIO archive that unpacks into rootfs before PID 1. If you build a custom initramfs with nasm or a tiny custom ELF loader embedded, then:

Bootloader loads initramfs
  → kernel decompresses it
  → kernel exec's /init    ← YOUR binary, which you wrote in assembly

Your /init can itself be a raw assembly ELF:
asm

; init.asm — valid PID 1 in assembly
BITS 64
SECTION .text
global _start
_start:
    ; you are now PID 1
    ; pipes and sockets not yet created by userspace
    ; but the kernel has pipefs and sockfs ready
    ; you can call sys_pipe, sys_socket right now
    mov rax, 22        ; sys_pipe2
    lea rdi, [rel fds]
    xor esi, esi
    syscall
    ...

This executes before systemd, before libc, before any C runtime — directly after the kernel hands off to userspace.
Relationship to Pipes/Sockets

Tying it back: the kernel has pipefs and sockfs ready before your assembly /init runs. So the first userspace syscall in your assembly binary could be sys_pipe2 or sys_socket — those kernel subsystems are already initialized by do_initcalls() which ran in C before execve("/init") was ever called.

The sequence in one line:

.S bootstrap → C kernel init (pipefs/sockfs ready) → execve(/init) → YOUR assembly runs → sys_pipe/sys_socket immediately available

So your assembly can call sys_pipe2 on its very first instruction if you want. The kernel already did all the work in C before handing control to you.

