AT&T vs Intel Syntax on x86
Short Answer

No — AT&T syntax is not the native x86 syntax. Intel syntax is. x86 was designed and documented by Intel, and Intel's own manuals use Intel syntax. AT&T syntax came from Unix tooling history and got baked into GAS (GNU Assembler), which is why the Linux kernel .S files use it. It's a toolchain choice, not a hardware requirement.
Why AT&T Ended Up in the Kernel

Bell Labs / AT&T Unix used their own assembler with their own syntax conventions in the 1970s–80s. When GCC was written and targeted x86, it emitted AT&T syntax because GAS (the GNU assembler) inherited that convention. The Linux kernel was built with GCC + GAS from day one (Linus, 1991), so the .S files follow GAS AT&T syntax.

It's purely historical toolchain inertia, not hardware design.
The Actual Differences
Feature	Intel Syntax	AT&T Syntax
Operand order	dst, src	src, dst
Registers	rax	%rax
Immediates	1000h or 0x1000	$0x1000
Memory	[rbp - 0x20]	-0x20(%rbp)
Size suffix	implied or DWORD PTR	movl, movq, movb
Jump target	jmp label	jmp label (same)

Same example in both:
asm

; Intel
mov rax, 1
mov rdi, 1
mov rsi, rbx
mov rdx, 14
syscall

# AT&T
movq $1, %rax
movq $1, %rdi
movq %rbx, %rsi
movq $14, %rdx
syscall

Exact same machine code output. The assembler translates either one to identical bytes.
Intel Syntax is Available on the Same Toolchain

You are not forced to use AT&T. GAS accepts Intel syntax with a directive:
asm

.intel_syntax noprefix

mov rax, 1
mov rdi, 1
syscall

NASM and YASM are dedicated Intel-syntax assemblers used heavily in security research, bootloaders, and embedded work — they never use AT&T syntax at all.

The Linux kernel itself has Intel syntax blocks in places where developers preferred it, using .intel_syntax noprefix / .att_syntax guards around sections.
What Intel's Own Documentation Uses

Every Intel Architecture manual (the SDM — Software Developer's Manual) uses Intel syntax exclusively:

MOV RAX, 1
PUSH RBP
MOV RBP, RSP

So when you're reading Intel docs and cross-referencing kernel .S files, you're constantly flipping operand order in your head — which is why most security researchers and low-level developers prefer NASM / Intel syntax and only deal with GAS AT&T when forced to by the kernel build system.
Bottom Line

    x86 is an Intel architecture → Intel syntax is the native/natural form
    AT&T syntax in kernel .S files is a GCC/GAS historical artifact
    The CPU doesn't know or care — both assemble to identical opcodes
    You can use Intel syntax via NASM, YASM, or .intel_syntax noprefix in GAS
    Most original x86 assembly literature (including Intel's own SDM) is Intel syntax

