x86 and ARM Preprocessor/Assembler Syntaxes Today
x86 — Active Syntaxes
1. Intel Syntax

The original. Used in Intel's own SDM documentation.

    NASM (Netwide Assembler) — most widely used in security/exploit work
    MASM (Microsoft Macro Assembler) — ships with Visual Studio, Windows kernel work
    YASM — NASM-compatible, now mostly superseded by NASM
    FASM (Flat Assembler) — self-hosting, used in OS dev and demoscene
    TASM (Turbo Assembler, Borland) — legacy, still used in some embedded/DOS shops

2. AT&T Syntax

    GAS (GNU Assembler, as) — Linux kernel, GCC backend
    GAS with .intel_syntax noprefix — switches GAS to Intel mid-file

3. HLA (High Level Assembly)

    Randall Hyde's HLA — Intel-like but with high-level constructs (procedure, if..then). Niche, academic use. Targets x86 only.

4. MASM-Compatible Variants

    JWASM — open MASM-compatible assembler
    UASM — fork of JWASM, actively maintained, targets x86/x86-64

ARM — Active Syntaxes
1. UAL (Unified Assembly Language)

The current ARM standard since ARMv6T2. One syntax covers both ARM (32-bit) and Thumb/Thumb-2 modes. All modern ARM documentation uses UAL.

    GAS ARM mode — uses UAL
    LLVM/Clang integrated assembler — uses UAL
    Keil MDK (armasm) — uses UAL with some proprietary extensions

2. Pre-UAL / Legacy ARM Syntax

Older GAS behavior before UAL was standardized. Still accepted by GAS with --warn flags. You'll see it in old kernel patches and legacy BSP code.
3. AArch64 (ARM64) Syntax

Technically UAL extended for 64-bit. Distinct enough in practice that it's treated separately:

    Register names change (x0–x30, w0–w30, sp, xzr)
    New instruction mnemonics (ldr x0, [x1, #8])
    Used in: Apple Silicon low-level code, Android kernel, server ARM work

4. Thumb / Thumb-2

Subset of UAL, 16/32-bit mixed encoding for code density. Embedded ARM (STM32, Nordic, etc.). Still UAL syntax, but instruction availability differs.
Commercial Assemblers with Proprietary Syntax Extensions
Product	Vendor	Platform	Notable Extension
MASM	Microsoft	x86/x64	PROTO, INVOKE, structured macros
KEIL armasm	ARM/Keil	ARM all	AREA, ENTRY, PRESERVE8 directives
IAR EWARM	IAR Systems	ARM/x86	MODULE, RSEG, IAR macro language
Green Hills MULTI	Green Hills	ARM/x86	Proprietary macro + inline asm dialect
Arm Compiler 6 (armclang)	ARM Ltd	AArch32/64	Clang integrated assembler, UAL + LLVM extensions
Count
Domain	Distinct Syntaxes
x86 commercial/active	Intel (NASM/MASM/FASM/UASM), AT&T (GAS), HLA — ~3 real syntaxes, ~6 assemblers
ARM commercial/active	UAL (32-bit), AArch64 UAL, Pre-UAL legacy, Keil/IAR proprietary extensions — ~3 core + vendor dialects

The real number is 2 syntaxes that matter for x86 (Intel and AT&T) and 1 that matters for ARM (UAL), with vendor assemblers adding macro/directive layers on top of those foundations without changing the core instruction syntax.

